    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1216</span><span class="rf-text">Abadir Umar ar-Rida founds the city according to the Fath Madinat Harar</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1332</span><span class="rf-text">Harar is first mentioned in a chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon I</a>, sending three governors to support Salih</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1520</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-muhammad">Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad</a> makes Harar the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1568</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-ibn-mujahid">Nur ibn Mujahid</a> dies of plague after famines and the building of the city wall</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">17th century</span><span class="rf-text">Ali ibn Da'ud founds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Harar and a dynasty that rules for two centuries</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1887</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Abdullahi at Chelenqo and conquers the walled city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">Italian forces capture Harar, which becomes the capital of the Harar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1941</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> Regiment captures Harar from the Italians</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/richard-francis-burton"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-francis-burton.jpg" alt="Richard Francis Burton" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/richard-francis-burton">Richard Francis Burton</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richard_Francis_Burton_by_Rischgitz,_1864.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rischgitz/Stringer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Harar stands on a hilltop on the Harar plateau in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, at an elevation of 1,885 metres and about 500 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>. When it was founded is unclear. Some accounts hold that Qurayshi settlers founded it in the late 7th century, others that it arose locally in the 10th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> had gained a foothold on the plateau by the 10th and 11th centuries through trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>, and by the 13th century it was the predominant religion of the region. The original inhabitants were probably the Harla people, and Harar was part of the Harla Kingdom's domain in the sixth century.</p><p>The semi-legendary Fath Madinat Harar names the saint Abadir Umar ar-Rida as the founder. He came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> with 405 other saints, among them Aw Barkhadle, Isma'il al-Jabarti and Umardin bin Qutbaddan, and founded the city about 612 AH (1216 CE). The Argobba, the Gaturi and the Harla accepted his leadership. The origin of the name is ambiguous: the archaeologist Timothy Insoll suggests that it comes from Harla, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-francis-burton">Richard Francis Burton</a> gave a plain south of the town or a particular tree. The first mention of the city is in the chronicles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon I</a>, which record that in 1332 Harar sent three governors to support Salih at the Battle of Das.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Harar&#8221; cites where it covers Etymology, Origins:</p><ul><li>Timothy Insoll, First Footsteps in the Archaeology of Harar, Ethiopia (Equinox Publishing Limited, 2017)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nur-ibn-mujahid"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nur-ibn-mujahid.jpg" alt="Nur ibn Mujahid" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nur-ibn-mujahid">Nur ibn Mujahid</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Streets_of_Harar,_Ethiopia_(2203217018).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A. Davey from Where I Live Now: Pacific Northwest, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walashma</a> established their residence at nearby Dakkar after their return from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> in 1415, and in 1520 Harar became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Imam Ahmad</a> ibn Ibrahim al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a>, called Gragn, later made it the centre of the campaigns that ravaged most of Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-ibn-mujahid">Nur ibn Mujahid</a> a large wall was built round the city, and the surrounding region was devastated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a>. Refugees flowed into the town, and in these years food and livestock rose steeply in price: a cow cost over 300 ashrafis.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of the Dawud dynasty Harar became a major religious centre and a source of Islamic proselytisation among the surrounding Oromo. Emir Abdullah made marriages with Oromo women for five of his sons, and the Oromo, now farming and trading, bought imported goods in the town's markets. The economic influence of Harar then reached from Shewa in the west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a> in the east, with some overseas connections. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> minted its own coins, certainly by 1789. In 1761 Ahmad I ibn Abi Bakr built the minaret of the grand mosque.</p><p>Burton visited in 1855 and counted about 8,000 inhabitants. He called it the emporium of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> trade. During the Egyptian occupation the walls were raised, a two-storey governor's house replaced the old palace of the emirs, the main mosque was rebuilt larger and a hospital was built near the south-western gate. Paultischke then gave the city roughly 40,000 inhabitants.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Harar&#8221; cites where it covers Ethnicity:</p><ul><li>Namhla Matshanda, Centres in the Periphery: Negotiating Territoriality and Identification in Harar and Jijiga from 1942 (The University of Edinburgh, 2014)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/menelik-ii.jpg" alt="Menelik II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Menelik_II_in_coronation_garb,_Emperor_of_Ethiopia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the ninth century Harar was under the Mahzumi dynasty's Sultanate of Shewa, and in the Islamic period it belonged to an alliance called the confederated states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walashma</a> rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> took the city in 1520, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Imam Ahmad</a> defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Abu Bakr in 1525 and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-din">Umar Din</a> as a puppet sultan. After Ahmad's defeat in 1543 his widow, Bati del Wambara, renewed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>, and in 1551 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-ibn-mujahid">Nur ibn Mujahid</a> married her and became ruler of Harar. Nur died in 1568, and a decade of instability followed under rulers who were deposed or killed in turn.</p><p>Muhammad Gasa, a descendant of Ahmad Gragn, moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aussa">Aussa</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> of Aussa, and from then on Harar was ruled by a local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">wazir</a>. In the 17th century Ali ibn Da'ud, who had been a district head and a minister, took the throne, declared independence and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Harar. His dynasty ruled for the next two centuries.</p><p>Egyptian troops under Muhammad Rauf Pasha took the city with no opposition, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> held it until 1885, when the emirate was restored under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Abdullahi. In 1887 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> conquered it and appointed his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Makonnen governor. Italian forces under Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rodolfo-graziani">Rodolfo Graziani</a> captured Harar on 8 May 1936, and it was the capital of the Harar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> Regiment took it on 29 March 1941.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Harar&#8221; cites where it covers Harar Emirate (Dawud dynasty 17th century onwards), Ethiopian rule (20th century):</p><ul><li>Avishai Ben-Dror, Emirate, Egyptian, Ethiopian: Colonial Experiences in Late Nineteenth-Century Harar (Syracuse University Press, 2018)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The old walled city covers about 60 hectares and has five quarters: Argobba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, Assum Bari, Badro Bari, Suqutat Bari and Asmadin Bari. They are divided into neighbourhoods called toya, often named after a Muslim shrine or a prominent tree, and there were 59 of them around 1975. Five ancient gates give access, and ramparts enclose the north, the east and part of the south. In 1840 most of the people were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harari">Hararis</a>, with a few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> and Arabian traders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. Somali scholars came to study in the city, among them Sheikh Madar, the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hargeisa">Hargeisa</a>, and in the 18th century sharifs migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. In the 19th century slaves of Sidama and Gurage origin were exported to the coast.</p><p>Harari houses, called gey gar, have a layout that dates from 1754, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Yusuf Abubakar. A walled compound holds several residences around a courtyard, with most windows facing inward. The main room, the gidir gar, has raised platforms called nadabas that serve as seats and beds, and the seat of honour is given by seniority. The walls are of local stone, with a mixture of pounded stones and clay as mortar and plaster. Women decorate the rooms with Harari basketry, and men meet at a barca to chew khat. Burton described the city as a walled city of stone houses with its own language and its own coinage.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon.jpg" alt="Charles George Gordon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon">Charles George Gordon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_George_Gordon_by_Freres.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Geruzet Frères - Belgian (active c. 1870-1889), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The wars with the Christian highlands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> repeatedly reached the city. In 1559 Ethiopians under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Hamalmal breached Harar and killed the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walashma</a> sultan, Barakat ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-din">Umar Din</a>. Under Nur the influx of refugees and the devastation of the region by the Oromo brought two severe famines, the second followed by a plague that killed Nur in 1568. On 1 September 1662 an Illamo Oromo raid reached the Asmadin gate and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Ali's son. After 1821 a quarrel between two brothers invited the Oromo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harari">Harari</a> politics, and forty villages around the city were destroyed in the civil war. In 1852 Emir Abu Bakr II had to pay tribute to the Oromo, which caused an economic crisis, and under Ahmad III the Oromo besieged the city for 18 months.</p><p>The Egyptian conquest brought brutal expeditions into the countryside in February and March 1876. The garrison of 160 officials, 3,411 soldiers and 5,000 of their wives and children made up a fifth of the population. The imposition of heavy taxes provoked protests, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-george-gordon">Charles George Gordon</a> removed Governor Rauf Pasha in 1884.</p><p>After Menelik's conquest in 1887 Jules Borelli reported that half the population fled. Shewan troops looted grain silos, and soldiers drove Hararis from their homes. Makonnen had the main mosque torn down and replaced by an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> church. Harar lost commerce when the railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a> was diverted away from it, and Dire Dawa was founded in 1902 as New Harar. A state-led crackdown in 1948 sent many Hararis out of the city for the first time in large numbers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jugol"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jugol.jpg" alt="Jugol" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jugol">Jugol</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_Gate,_Harar_Jugol_(14464345823).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rod Waddington from Kergunyah, Australia, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Harar is called the City of Saints in Arabic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>, in listing Harar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugol">Jugol</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2006, considered it the fourth holiest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and counted 82 mosques, three dating from the 10th century, and 102 shrines. Its inscription rests on a 16th-century wall, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harari">Harari</a> urban fabric and its traditions, though plastering of houses, metal doors in place of wooden ones and television antennas have gradually affected the authenticity of the historic fabric.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-rimbaud">Arthur Rimbaud</a> lived in the city during the Egyptian period and returned in 1888 to trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, musk and skins until a fatal illness forced him back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. A house said to have been his residence is now a museum. According to Burton, Harar was the birthplace of the khat plant, and the original domesticated coffee plant is also said to have come from there. The Harari language, long used in the city's government and archives, was encouraged in official use after the early 1990s.</p><p>Harar is the capital of the Harari Region. The 2007 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 99,368 people in the city, of whom 40.55% were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a>, 28.14% <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> and 11.83% Harari, and 48.54% reported that they were Muslim and 44.56% Christian. The Harari were a majority inside the walled city under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> but are now under 15% there.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Harar is a walled hilltop city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, founded according to tradition about 1216 and made capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> in 1520. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> conquered it in 1887. Its old city is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</em></p>

